  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "-er and -ir verbs",
      "body": "The other two groups work just like *-ar* verbs: take off the ending, add the person endings. *-er* and *-ir* verbs share almost all their endings — they only differ for *nosotros*.",
      "table": {
        "head": ["Pronoun", "comer (to eat)", "vivir (to live)"],
        "rows": [
          ["*yo*", "*como*", "*vivo*"],
          ["*tú*", "*comes*", "*vives*"],
          ["*él / ella / usted*", "*come*", "*vive*"],
          ["*nosotros / nosotras*", "*comemos*", "*vivimos*"],
          ["*ustedes*", "*comen*", "*viven*"],
          ["*ellos / ellas*", "*comen*", "*viven*"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "The pattern at a glance",
      "body": "- *-ar*: *-o, -as, -a, -amos, -an*\n- *-er*: *-o, -es, -e, -emos, -en*\n- *-ir*: *-o, -es, -e, -imos, -en*\n\nThe *yo* form always ends in *-o*. The trick is remembering the vowel: *a* for -ar verbs, *e* for -er and -ir verbs, except *nosotros* of -ir verbs, which keeps the *i*: *vivimos, escribimos, abrimos*."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Useful -er and -ir verbs",
      "body": "*Comer* (to eat), *beber* (to drink), *leer* (to read), *aprender* (to learn), *comprender* (to understand), *correr* (to run), *vender* (to sell); *vivir* (to live), *escribir* (to write), *abrir* (to open), *recibir* (to receive), *compartir* (to share).\n\n*Aprender a* + infinitive means \"to learn to\": *Aprendo a cocinar* (I'm learning to cook). In much of Latin America, *tomar* is more common than *beber* for drinks: *tomo agua*."
    }
  ],
